cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "This work is under anonymous double-blind review. Please update this file with the full citation upon acceptance."
title: "Hidden Hunger, Hidden Uncertainty: Auditing the Evidential Blind Spots of Climate-Nutrition Models"
authors:
  - name: "Anonymous"
date-released: 2026-01-01
license: MIT
repository-code: "<add-repository-url-here>"
abstract: >
  Elevated atmospheric CO2 measurably lowers the protein, iron, and zinc
  content of staple cereals, a phenomenon the plant science literature
  calls hidden hunger. This work shows that the entire experimental
  evidence base for this effect in cereal grains rests on 76 Fe/Zn/N
  observations from just 11 independent field sites in 6 countries, none
  in South Asia or Sub-Saharan Africa, and introduces evidential distance,
  a training-free diagnostic that measures how far a target region's
  climate covariates sit from this real experimental evidence.
